---
name: dev-task
version: 3.2.2
description: lead dev assignment for a planned dev bead in atm-bd-orchestration. The bead is the assignment; task_id is the bead id.
format: xml
required_variables:
  - task_id
  - sprint
  - stack
  - pr_target
  - worktree_path
  - branch
  - primary_checkout
  - phase_root
  - lead
  - cc
  - test_command
  - policy_path
metadata:
  type: dev-task
  tags:
    - stage:dev
    - orchestration:beads
  workflow:
    scope:
      kind: sprint
      variable: sprint
    state: dev-start
    stage: dev
    transition: start
---
<atm-task id="{{ task_id }}" sprint="{{ sprint }}">
  <description><![CDATA[Implement dev bead `{{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }}` (sprint {{ sprint | string | cdata_escape }}). `bd show {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }}` is the assignment; its description, design, acceptance criteria and metadata are authoritative.]]></description>

  <worktree><![CDATA[{{ worktree_path | string | cdata_escape }}]]></worktree>
  <branch><![CDATA[{{ branch | string | cdata_escape }}]]></branch>
  <stack><![CDATA[{{ stack | string | cdata_escape }}]]></stack>
  <pr-target><![CDATA[{{ pr_target | string | cdata_escape }}]]></pr-target>
  <stack-discipline><![CDATA[`{{ branch | string | cdata_escape }}` is cut from and rebases only onto `origin/{{ pr_target | string | cdata_escape }}`. Work only on this branch. {{ lead | string | cdata_escape }} is the stack writer: never run `gh stack link`, `sync`, `rebase` or `merge`. Once linked the layer is frozen; later changes are fix beads.]]></stack-discipline>

  <workflow>
    <step id="a"><![CDATA[Before claiming, run `git fetch origin && git rebase origin/{{ pr_target | string | cdata_escape }}` in the worktree (a conflict is part of the fix), then `{{ primary_checkout | string | cdata_escape }}/.claude/skills/atm-bd-orchestration/scripts/assignment-gates.py dev --root {{ phase_root | string | cdata_escape }} --bead {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }} --pr-target {{ pr_target | string | cdata_escape }} --worktree {{ worktree_path | string | cdata_escape }}` from the worktree (scripts come from the primary checkout, never the branch). Each check, its command and its refusal code: (1) plan valid: `{{ primary_checkout | string | cdata_escape }}/.claude/skills/atm-beads/scripts/validate-plan --root {{ phase_root | string | cdata_escape }}` exits 0, else `PLAN_INVALID`; (2) ready: `bd ready -n 0 --json` lists the bead, else `NOT_READY`; (3) claimable without `--force`: `bd show {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }} --json` shows status `open` and assignee empty or `$ATM_IDENTITY`, else `UNCLAIMABLE`; (4) target: `metadata.pr_target` equals `{{ pr_target | string | cdata_escape }}`, else `PR_TARGET_MISMATCH`; (5) difficulty: your `atm members --json` model is in `DIFFICULTY_MODELS[metadata.difficulty]`, else `DIFFICULTY_MISMATCH`; (6) base: `git merge-base --is-ancestor origin/{{ pr_target | string | cdata_escape }} HEAD` exits 0, else `WRONG_BASE`. `GATE_CANNOT_RUN` when a command fails. Reuse an existing workflow class bead for the same failure signature: append the task id, head, command and failure evidence, and cite the class id in the refusal. If no class matches, report the signature to {{ lead | string | cdata_escape }} for classification and cite that message instead; do not create a per-task shadow or delay the refusal. On success claim with `bd update {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }} --claim --actor "$ATM_IDENTITY"`, then start `atm task start {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }} "<one line: what you will do first>"`.]]></step>
    <step id="a1"><![CDATA[When it prints `NOT_READY`, do not claim the bead or start the task. Find the root cause with `bd show {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }}` (status, assignee), `bd blocked --json | jq --arg b {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }} '.[] | select(.id == $b)'` (its open blockers), and `bd show <blocker>` for each (status, assignee, what it waits on). Report with `atm send {{ lead | string | cdata_escape }} --stdin`: the bead, why it is not ready, and which bead or agent has to move. Then wait; {{ lead | string | cdata_escape }} re-dispatches or cancels the task.]]></step>
    <step id="c"><![CDATA[Read `bd show {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }}` before coding. It is authoritative for scope, deliverables, deletion targets, validation, and acceptance criteria; `metadata.owned_paths` is your file fence. If this assignment and the bead differ, the bead wins; report the mismatch. Before coding, read every id in `metadata.requirements` and `metadata.adrs` in the governing documents: your change must satisfy each, and QA checks it against them. `NONE` means the plan declares none; if the work touches a requirement or ADR anyway, report it to {{ lead | string | cdata_escape }} before coding past it.]]></step>
    <step id="c0"><![CDATA[Before editing, read the QA policy `{{ policy_path | string | cdata_escape }}` and every guideline it names for the languages this task touches; reviewers score against them.]]></step>
    <step id="d"><![CDATA[Work from the bead's deliverables and acceptance criteria; nothing outside its scope. Read the complete plan and enumerate all tasks that must be completed in the primary and any child beads; create an itemized private checklist outside the tracked tree with every task identified, and work through the checklist one item at a time. Commit on `{{ branch | string | cdata_escape }}` as items land and push early (`git push -u origin {{ branch | string | cdata_escape }}` first) so the work is never only local. A push is not a close and needs no report.]]></step>
    <step id="e"><![CDATA[Finish every item, then go through the checklist again one item at a time and confirm each is fully met; do not weaken a test, skip a criterion, or leave a placeholder. Run `{{ test_command | string | cdata_escape }}` and the bead's validation commands until they pass with zero failures.]]></step>
    <step id="f"><![CDATA[Rebase only onto the declared target: `git fetch origin && git rebase origin/{{ pr_target | string | cdata_escape }}`, re-run `{{ test_command | string | cdata_escape }}`, then `git push --force-with-lease origin {{ branch | string | cdata_escape }}`. Record the full head SHA and `{{ pr_target | string | cdata_escape }}`.]]></step>
    <step id="g"><![CDATA[Close the bead and task together: `bd close {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }} --reason "dev complete at <short sha>" --actor "$ATM_IDENTITY"` and `atm task close {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }} completed --template .claude/skills/atm-bd-orchestration/templates/dev-complete.md.j2 --vars <scratch>/dev-complete-{{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }}-vars.json`. Build the vars from the template's `required_variables`: `task_id` and `sprint` unchanged from this assignment, `commit` the pushed head and `rebased_onto` the top from step f (the lead reads both), the deliverable inventory, and the exact gate output lines; for deletion or cleanup work add the before/after targeted symbol or code-path inventory as `inventory_md`. Write the vars file outside the repository and never commit it. Only this close frees your queue: the next assignment never starts until `{{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }}` is closed, and a plain `atm send` report does not close it.{% if cc and cc != lead %} Copy a one-line summary to `{{ cc | string | cdata_escape }}` with a plain `atm send {{ cc | string | cdata_escape }} --stdin`.{% endif %}]]></step>
    <step id="g1"><![CDATA[If the bead cannot be completed at all, reuse an existing workflow class bead for the same failure signature: append the task id, head, command and failure evidence, and cite the class id in the refusal. If no class matches, report the signature to {{ lead | string | cdata_escape }} for classification and cite that message instead; create no per-task shadow and do not delay the refusal. Declare failure with `bd update {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }} --status blocked --assignee "" --append-notes "DEV_CANNOT_COMPLETE; workflow class or escalation <reference>: <reason>" --actor "$ATM_IDENTITY"` (never `bd close`: a closed dev bead releases its sanity check) and `atm task close {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }} refused --template .claude/skills/atm-bd-orchestration/templates/task-refused.md.j2 --vars <scratch>/refused-{{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }}-vars.json`. An unfinished deliverable is a failure, not a completion.]]></step>
    <step id="h"><![CDATA[After the close, read ATM again: the next queued task starts only now.]]></step>
  </workflow>
</atm-task>
